          "question": "What actually happens in the airways during an asthma flare-up?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nBased on the asthma reference in this corpus (the \"Asthma\" document), the airways are **inflamed**, and the **muscle around them is prone to tightening**, which narrows the airways and limits airflow. The narrowing varies over time and is at least partly reversible. The document says this variability is what defines asthma, and that symptoms like wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough often get worse at night or early in the morning, and can be set off by triggers.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not explain what changes step by step during a flare-up, such as which cells are involved or how the swelling and tightening build up. I can't fill that gap from memory. A clinician or a more detailed asthma resource would be the place to ask.\n\nIf you or someone near you is struggling to breathe, has blue lips, or cannot speak in full sentences, seek emergency care right away.",
